> Here is a summary of the files I have created under a large project to assess climate damages in the UK:
> ===
> Reproduction code for "Towards comprehensive assessments of national climate damages: an application to the United Kingdom"
> 
> All code is encluded. The code in `src1` was used in the initial work,
> and `src2` was used where COACCH and CCRA3 was incorporated.
> 
>  - /Users/admin/research/ukcp/src1/topdown/graphs.R: Visualizing Climate Damages Across Scenarios
> 
> This R script processes and visualizes economic impact data related to climate change. It begins by setting the working directory and loading data from three sources: Dell, Jones, Olken (2012), Burke et al. (2015), and Kahn et al. (2019). It combines these datasets and labels them according to scenarios (SSP1-2.6 and SSP3-7.0) and periods. Using the 'dplyr' library, the script groups data to calculate welfare-equivalent damages (% GDP) for the UK and rest of the world (ROW). Confidence intervals are also computed. The script uses 'ggplot2' to generate several plots that depict these damages over different scenarios and time periods, highlighting UK-only and ROW impacts. Additional data handling includes the formulation of combined datasets and the use of faceted and flipped axis plots to effectively display the results.
> 
>  - /Users/admin/research/ukcp/src1/topdown/combine.R: Combining and Analyzing National Climate Impacts
> 
> This script processes climate impact data for different scenarios (ssp126, ssp370) and years (2020, 2050, 2090), using projections from multiple studies (`burkeetal`, `djo`, `kahnetal`). It categorizes impacts into periods, averages them, and combines results at the administrative level (ADM0). The script calculates the mean impacts, and fractional losses for each scenario and period. GDP impacts are estimated by calculating the trade loss for the UK and global scenarios. It then summarizes these results, generating tables and maps, and saves the results to files for further analysis. The data is prepared to understand national and global climate damage assessments, emphasizing the UK, and helps in policy planning by showing potential economic impacts over different time horizons.
> 
>  - /Users/admin/research/ukcp/src1/topdown/burkeetal.R: Climate Impact Simulation and Mapping
> 
> This code script is part of an analysis for projecting climate impacts. It sets up parameters using statistical methods, specifically drawing coefficients from a multivariate normal distribution to simulate climate impacts based on temperature anomalies. The `setup` function initializes the coefficients depending on the scenario and `run_id`, while `simulate` calculates the impacts using temperature data. The results are generated through `country.project`, saved, and then processed to compute cumulative impacts. Mapping functions visually represent the data. The analysis concludes by calculating final results, formatting them for output, and adjusting confidence interval measures within the projections. The script manages data storage and retrieval, producing a CSV file with summarized projections, and ensures compatibility with subsequent analysis by adjusting the order and naming of confidence interval measures.
> 
>  - /Users/admin/research/ukcp/src1/topdown/driver.R: Projecting and Mapping Climate Impact on Country-level GDP
> 
> The script in `/Users/admin/research/ukcp/src1/topdown/driver.R` processes climate model data to simulate the impacts of climate change on different countries, particularly focusing on temperature changes from historical baselines. It mainly involves loading climate data, calculating temperature adjustments using CMIP6 scenarios, and assessing country-specific impacts over time. Key computations include adjusting national temperatures based on global mean surface temperature (GMST) differences and spreading these adjustments over projected years using provided shares. These simulations inform future economic impacts, specifically the change in GDP per capita. The script also includes functionalities to visualize results through map projections, depicting expected GDP losses under various scenarios. Customizable functions for running simulations and generating visualizations make this script adaptable for different climate models or scenarios. Additionally, the code allows for the incorporation of various impact measures (mean, 5% quantile, and 95% quantile) to illustrate potential ranges of economic losses.
> 
>  - /Users/admin/research/ukcp/src1/topdown/catastrophic.R: Catastrophic Climate Damage Simulation Code
> 
> This script analyzes national climate damages by simulating potential outcomes based on climate change data. It begins by setting the working directory and establishing the mean (\( \mu \)) and standard error (se) for a random coefficient used in damage calculations. The dataset, containing temperature projections from different scenarios, is loaded from a CSV file. For each unique run of the simulation, it applies a random damage coefficient drawn from a normal distribution with defined mean and standard error to calculate square-law damages. The script classifies the data into different periods (2011-2030, 2041-2060, and 2081-2100) before summarizing the calculated damages by scenario, period, and run_id using the `dplyr` package. Finally, the summarized results are saved to an R data file.
> 
>  - /Users/admin/research/ukcp/src1/topdown/togdp.R: Assessing Trade and GDP Impacts from Brexit Scenarios
> 
> The script from `/Users/admin/research/ukcp/src1/topdown/togdp.R` analyzes the economic impacts of different Brexit scenarios and climate change on the UK's GDP, exports, and imports. The code utilizes data sources from Latorre et al. 2019 and Dhingra et al. 2017, comparing "Soft Brexit" and "Hard Brexit" across various parameters. Linear regression is performed to model GDP change based on export and import variations. The `calc.tradeloss` function estimates trade-driven GDP loss using Comtrade data, while `calc.finals` computes GDP loss under future climate scenarios (ssp126, ssp370) across different time periods and confidence intervals. Finally, `final2tbl` prepares a comparative table summarizing the GDP impacts, demonstrating losses due to trade and climate changes, and differences between scenarios using a formatted table output.
> 
>  - /Users/admin/research/ukcp/src1/topdown/kahnetal.R: Temperature Impact Assessment for the UK
> 
> This R script, based on temperature data and economic modeling, aims to project the impact of temperature variations on the UK's economy. The model estimates temperature-related damages by computing differences from a 30-year mean temperature, generating coefficients using a multivariate normal distribution with specific parameters (`gamma`, `gammavcv`). The `setup` function initializes model conditions based on scenario and run ID, while the `simulate` function calculates climate impacts by analyzing temperature differences and updating a rolling historical temperature matrix. Impacts on different regions are determined, aggregated, and saved for further analysis. The results are further processed to calculate economic impacts relative to GDP using another script. Finalized results are written to a CSV file and adjusted for specific measures of confidence intervals, which are stored and reformatted for presentation.
> 
>  - /Users/admin/research/ukcp/src1/topdown/djo.R: UK Climate Damage Assessment Code
> 
> This script primarily handles data extraction and analysis related to GDP impacts on poorer countries over time. It sets up the working directory and sources essential scripts (`driver.R` and `togdp.R`). The script reads and cleans GDP per capita data for multiple countries, focusing on data starting from the 1970s. It identifies countries classified as 'poors' based on GDP per capita being below the median. A simulation function calculates impacts using coefficients for poorer countries, and results are generated through the `country.project` function. Results are processed to obtain final summaries, which are then saved as a CSV file. The script also reorganizes confidence interval labels ('ci95' and 'ci05') in the final dataset before tabulating and saving them.
> 
